From: Paul-Louis Charrière <plcharriere@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: nf_flow_table: tear down flows on bridge FDB moves

A flow whose destination is behind a bridge keeps the bridge port it was
created for.  A software flow with direct transmission sends on that port
itself, and a hardware flow has the port, or for Wi-Fi the station's WDMA
target, programmed into the offload engine.  When the destination moves
to another port, for example a station roaming between two access points
of the same bridge, the flow keeps sending to the old port.  Offloaded
traffic keeps the flow alive, so this lasts until the sender gives up.

When nft or xt_FLOWOFFLOAD creates a flow, record the bridge port its
destination resolved to on the forward path, along with the device the
lookup started from and the MAC address the bridge looked up.  The driver
resolves the target of a neighbour tuple itself when it installs the
hardware rule, so check the port again at that point: record it if the
bridge did not know the destination when the flow was created, and tear
the flow down instead of installing the rule if the destination has moved
since.

When the bridge reports a learned or moved address, rescan the flowtables
and resolve the recorded ports of the flows to that address again, keeping
up to 16 addresses per scan and resolving all recorded ports, at most once
a second, past that.  Tear a flow down only when its address is now
forwarded to another port.  An address missing from the FDB, e.g. aged out
while its traffic bypasses the bridge, is no evidence of a move and is left
alone.  Software flows that transmit through the bridge device are switched
by the bridge on every packet and are skipped.

After tearing down hardware flows, run the garbage collector at once and
wait for the hardware deletes.  Never queue or run a hardware add for a
flow being torn down, and keep a dead flow until the offload work queued
on it has run, so that a late add can neither install a rule that nothing
removes nor run on a freed flow.

Based-on-patch-by: Bo-Cun Chen <bc-bocun.chen@mediatek.com>
Based-on-patch-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
